Package.json is the configuration for npm
npm is a package manager but don't have a full form
Bundler like webpack bundles your code so that it can be pushed to production
^ minor upgrade will be installae automatically
~ major version upgrade will be installed automatically
package-lock.json keep an exact track of version of packages
node modules contains all the code npm fetches for a package while installing
Transitive Dependencies are there in node modules 
There are dev depnecies installed with npm install -D parcel
There are also production dependencies 
Transitive depnedencies -> Like all dependencies have their own package.json having their own dependecies 
npm init creates the package.json
npx parcel index.html -> Igniting the App
npm install installs the package
npx executes the package
React is a Javascript code hosted on npm
CDN links is not a preferred way to bring React 
npm install React
Parcle creates Dev buils, starts local server and also and Hot Module Replacement i.e anything chnaged page refreshes by itself
Parcel uses a file watching algorithm for HMR written in C++.
Parcel gives faster builds because of Caching in folder parcel-cache
parcel do image optimization also as most expensive thing is to load images 
Parcel compress, minify, bundle, consistent hashing, code splitting, good error handling, differential bundling
Parcel does Tree Shaking also that is remove Unused code
Parcel has a dev and prod build separatley
For production build npx parcel build index.html
For dev build : npx parcel index.html
browserslist -> compatible with browsers
npm run start or npm start same thing
in package.json check for start script to see the command to run the project 
npx is not needed because npm puts the node modules path at 0th position
React.create element just create objects which then ReactDom.render renders as html
JSX is just a javascript syntax 
JSX is not the html written inside Javascript but JSX is a html like syntax
JSX is not a valid Javascript as browser, js engines can't read this code 
JSX code is transpiled by Parcel(using Babel) before it reaches js engines
Babel is a Javascript Compiler -> Job to convert JSX to JS code
JSX --> Converted to React.createElement --> Then rendered as HTML
Parcel is like the manager of Babel.
html use class and jsx className
Attributes to JSX are given in camelCase
Wrap JSX in () if you are writing in multiple lines

React components two ways to write : 
Class Based components - OLD way 
Function Based components - NEW -> A Javascript function that returns some piece of JSX => Name Capital letter 

A functional Component rendered like root.render(< HeadingComponent />)
Rendering Component inside Component is called Component Composition.
JSX is a mix of HTML and Javascript.
You can write js in {} in JSX 
You can put ReactElement in ReactComponent or vice-versa or can do any sort of mix and match 
JSX takes care of injection attacks 
JSX stops cross-scripting attacks as it sanitizes the data whatever you write in {} in code 
You can call a Function also in JSX so basically React Components are also JS Functions and they can be called inside like functions inside JSX
In jsx we write inline css like :
const styleCard = {
    backgroundColor : "red"
}

<h1 style={styleCard}> So style has to be given like JS object

React has props means properties
Since React components are functions so props are just arguments passing to a function.

<ResCard cuisine="north india" res="chulbul dhaba" />
props will be passed as objects
const ResCard = (props)=>{}
or destructuring on the fly
const ResCard = ({cuisine, res}) => {}

Config Driven UI :

resList.map((restraunt)=> <Restraunt key={id}/>) //Iterating over the array 
while using map always make sure to pass the unique key. Otherwise everything will be re rendered
React itself says that never use index as keys
"React reuses component instances based on the key."

You can name a file as Header.js or Header.jsx same thing
for export - export default componentName
import - import Header from './components/Header

export default only one thing can be exported from a file but when multiple things needs to be exported from the same file you have to use named export : 
export const CDN_URL = "https://abc.com"
For doing named imports use curly braces : import {CDN_URL} from '.././components
React Hooks are the morkak Javascript functionS.
Fcebook employees have written these
There are many react hooks but mostly used out of the are : 
useSate(()=>{})
useEffect(()=>{})

import {useState} from "react"

usestate is used to create a state variable
State variable the maintains the state of the component
import { useState } from "react"
const [variablename] = useState([])
variable created using setState cannot be modified like other variables.It is done like:
const [variableName, setVariableName] = useState([]);
setVariableName(filteredList)
Whenever a state variable changes -> React will rerender the component
React is so good at DOM Manipulations
React keep Data layer in sync with UI layer 
so like as just you change restrauntList variable UI will be rerendered to show the updated list on UI 
React uses Reconciliation Algorithm (React Fibre): 
Virtual DOM is not an Actual DOM.It it like an object containing React Elements 
Diff Algorith compares the old virtual DOM and latest virtual DOM 
and then updates the Actual DOM 
In react 16 a new algo to called React Fibre for DOM Update
Virtual DOM is an object representation of DOM
Whenever a state change is there then react compares the virtual DOM and re renders the UI
React is doinf effecient DOM Maniplation because it has virtual DOM hence it is faster
Virtual DOM is the object representation of HTML
//Array destructuring
const ans = useState(resList);
const [res, setRes] = ans 

Monolith Architecture -> Where all service like API, UI, DB etc are there in single project
Microservices Architecture -> Where all services are separate.Like separation of concerns
Single Responsibility Principle is followed in Microservices architecture.
In microservice architecture every service can be written in any language/framework
All the services can be deployed on different domains and ports 
When we call API to show data in our web page there are two approaches : 
1. Page Loads -> api call(some 500 ms) -> and then only page will render 
2. Page loads -> Render(whatever minimum you can) -> make API call -> Re render the page with updated content.
Second approach is better approach as it helps in better UX.
React has one of the best Rendering algos.It re renders very fast

useEffect hook => so Basically once your that particular component will be rendered the the callback function of useEffect will be called.
So if you want anything do be done once render is done, do make useEffect use.

useEffect(()=>{}, [])
Two argument it takes callback function and a dependency array 

//fetch is functon not given by javascript it is given by JS engine in browsers
const fetchData = async()=>{
    const data = await fetch("url") //fetch returns a promise that's why using async await 
    const json  = await data.json()
    setRestraunt(data.cards[2])
}

